Slide 1 – Title
Comprehensive Data Analytics Framework for Project Management

Executive Overview

Slide 2 – Objective
Predict employee workloads and project demands

Optimize resource allocation

Identify gaps proactively to ensure efficient utilization

Slide 3 – Why Data Analytics in PM?
Transforms past data into forward-looking insights

Enables proactive rather than reactive decision-making

Balances productivity, timelines, resources, and budget

Slide 4 – Benefits (Merits)
Proactive resource allocation

Enhanced efficiency & utilization

Early risk detection and mitigation

Cost reduction through optimized staffing

Objective, data-driven decisions

Slide 5 – Limitations (Demerits)
Depends on data quality

Forecasts carry probabilistic uncertainty

Requires specialized skills and maintenance

Slide 6 – Key Data Sources
STR_cleaned.xlsx – employee hours & billing status

CoreAlm_master.xlsx – project tasks & effort logs

Milestone_tracker.xlsx – milestone dates & status

Employee_Whitelist.xlsx – authorized employees

Slide 7 – Analytics Workflow – Overview
Data preparation & cleaning

Weekly aggregation & median benchmarks

Feature engineering (lags, rolling averages)

Predictive modeling (XGBoost / Gradient Boosting)

Resource forecasting & milestone alignment

Reporting & stakeholder insights

Slide 8 – Data Preparation
Robust ISO-week parser for consistency

Standardize email / date formats

Classify hours as billable vs non-billable

Slide 9 – Aggregation & Analysis
Weekly summaries per employee & project

Dynamic median of project-week hours as benchmark

Why median? Robust to outliers

Slide 10 – Feature Engineering & Forecasting
Lag features: capture temporal dependencies

Rolling averages: smooth fluctuations & reveal trends

Models: XGBoost / Gradient Boosting for structured data

Iteratively minimize error to improve accuracy

Slide 11 – Resource Forecast & Utilization Targets
Weekly predictions of available hours & shortfalls

Utilization benchmarks highlight over/under allocation

Identify additional resources or task redistribution needs

Slide 12 – Milestone Tracking
Forecast milestone completion based on resource outlook

Prioritize critical milestones to stay on schedule

Slide 13 – Evaluation Metrics
MAE – average absolute error

RMSE – penalizes larger errors

R² – proportion of variance explained

MAPE – error as percentage of actuals

Slide 14 – Practical Applications
Align staffing with upcoming workload peaks

Flag resource shortages early

Support strategic decisions on budgeting & hiring

Enhance on-time milestone delivery

Slide 15 – Conclusion
Analytics adds foresight to project management

Benefits outweigh challenges when data quality is managed

Framework enables continuous, data-driven improvement
